// main.fluid: an example of the top layer of FluidXPro3
//
//   main.fluid --(fluid build: LLM)--> main.fx3 --(fx3 build)--> IR --> C++ / Python
//
//   - .fluid is where you write intent, in plain English
//   - .fx3 is generated, strict and checked, and you CAN edit it by hand
//     (see FLUID_SPEC.md for how edits are kept)
//   - The target language is chosen by you (`target` below)
//   - How often the builder stops to ask you is set with --ask=soft|mid|hard,
//     or per block with an `ask` line
//   - How the generated code looks (modularity, safety, comments, ...) is set
//     in fluid.config (JSON), or per block with a `with` line
//
// Keywords start a line and are lowercase. Everything else is prose.
// The full keyword list is in FLUID_SPEC.md.


app Expense Tracker
    Keep track of my daily expenses, show monthly summaries,
    and warn me before I go over my budget.
    Runs as a small web server, and can also be used from the terminal.

target python
    // or: target cpp. Required, because the coder picks the language.

ask mid
    // the default level for this file. --ask on the command line overrides it


uses
    sqlite database in "expenses.db"
    email, to send me warnings

secrets
    TRACKER_EMAIL     my email address
    TRACKER_KEY       the key every web request must send
    // read from the environment, never written into the generated code or logs

settings
    monthly budget is 20,000 rupees
    warn me at 80% of the budget
    port 8080


// ---- words: what my terms mean ------------------------------------------------
// Words defined here mean the same thing EVERYWHERE in the file, so the LLM
// doesn't have to guess.

words                // aliases: glossary, terms, definitions
    "this month"     the calendar month in my local time zone, not the last 30 days
    "spent"          the total of expenses that are not cancelled
    "warning level"  80% of the monthly budget
    "big expense"    any single expense above 5,000 rupees


// ---- data ---------------------------------------------------------------------

thing Expense
    amount: money, always more than zero
    category: one of food, travel, bills, fun, other
    note: short text, optional
    date: defaults to today
    cancelled: yes or no, starts as no

thing Summary
    month
    spent
    spent per category, biggest first
    percent of budget used


// ---- functions ----------------------------------------------------------------

to add expense (amount, category, note) -> the saved expense
    Save a new expense dated today.
    If "spent" this month now passes the warning level, send me a warning email,
    but at most once per month.

to cancel expense (id)
    Mark it cancelled. If it doesn't exist, say so clearly.
    ask hard
    with safety paranoid, comments detailed
        // money is involved: ask about anything unclear, even with --ask=soft,
        // and generate the most defensive, most explained code

to monthly summary (month) -> Summary
    Work out what I spent that month, grouped by category.

    example
        given food 500 and travel 1500 in March 2026
        monthly summary of March 2026
        -> spent 2000, travel first, budget used 10%

to split bill (total, people) -> a list of amounts
    Divide the total as evenly as possible. The amounts must add up exactly.
    exact
        ```fx3
        function[<int, int>|<vector<int>>] splitBill = Function(int total, int people) {
            int share = total ~/ people;
            int left  = total % people;
            return range(0, people).map(share + (if (currVal < left) 1 else 0));
        }
        ```


// ---- check: a decision written as a table (becomes a fx3 checker) -----------

check how worried should I be (spent, budget)
    under 50% of budget        -> relaxed
    50% to 80%                 -> careful
    80% to 100%                -> worried
    over 100%                  -> over budget


// ---- flow: steps in order (becomes a fx3 pipeline) ---------------------------

flow import bank statement (csv file)
    1. read the CSV rows
    2. turn each row into an Expense; skip rows that aren't expenses
    3. guess the category from the description          (ai)
    4. leave out any expense that is already saved (same date, amount and note)
    5. save the rest, and tell me how many were added and how many skipped
    if any step fails, save nothing at all


// ---- ai: calls the model WHILE THE PROGRAM RUNS (not at build time) -------------

ai category guesser (description) -> category
    Pick the best category for a bank description such as "SWIGGY*ORDER 2231".
    If unsure, answer "other".
    // `(ai)` in the flow above refers to this. It runs at runtime,
    // so its cost and speed are visible.


// ---- when: react to changes in the data -----------------------------------------

when a big expense is added
    Send me an email straight away, with the amount and the note.

when the month changes
    Email me last month's summary.


// ---- web API ----------------------------------------------------------------

web
    GET    /expenses             -> this month's expenses, newest first
    POST   /expenses             -> add expense using the JSON body
    DELETE /expenses/{id}        -> cancel expense
    GET    /summary/{month}      -> monthly summary
    POST   /import               -> import bank statement from an uploaded file
    Every request needs the header X-Key, which must equal TRACKER_KEY.


// ---- page: a simple screen (generated HTML, served by `web`) --------------------

page dashboard at /
    A header with this month's "spent" and a progress bar toward the budget.
    The bar's colour comes from `how worried should I be`.
    Below it: a pie chart by category, then the last 10 expenses.
    A button that opens a small form to add an expense.


// ---- terminal -----------------------------------------------------------------

command "add 250 food lunch with team"
    Work out the amount, category and note from the words, then add expense.

command "summary"
    Show this month's summary as a neat table.


// ---- things that happen on their own -----------------------------------------

on start
    Make sure the database and tables exist.

every day at 9pm
    If I haven't added anything today, send me a gentle reminder.

on error
    Log it with the time and the request, and reply with a friendly message.


// ---- rules: hard limits (each becomes a check in fx3) ---------------------------

// A rule the compiler can't verify is a warning by default. Tag a line to change
// that: [error] = fail the build, [off] = don't report it.

rules
    money is stored as whole paise, never as decimals
    expenses are never deleted, only cancelled                [error]
    the web API must answer within 200 ms
    no more than 10 emails per day, in total


// ---- never: what this program must NOT do or include ----------------------------

never
    send my data to any service other than email
    add user accounts or logins (it's only for me)


// ---- tests ------------------------------------------------------------------

test going over the warning level sends one email
    given a budget of 1000 and 750 spent this month
    add expense 100 food, then add expense 10 food
    -> exactly one warning email is sent

test cancelled expenses don't count
    add expense 500 fun, then cancel it
    monthly summary -> spent 0
